  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124445,
      "id": "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340",
      "title": "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c2dd6d53-f379-59ce-9501-ada3138f4833",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{8}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{4}{zx}-z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇f(x,y,z)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{8}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{4}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{4}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8z+4y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{8}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{4}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8+2x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{8}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{4}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{8z+4y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{8+2x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{4+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8z+4y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{8+2x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{4+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8z+4y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{8+2x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{4+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-4.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4+{z}^{2}(-4)}{{z}^{2}(-4)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-4$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8z+4y}{(-4)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ 8z+4y & =0 \\\\ y & =-2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-2$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=2.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-4,-2,1)$ and $(-4,2,-1).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2d5d48ae-db06-55a8-951b-4d7355c8e06b",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "922bd0b2-1e22-5644-b511-e22fff5d2e31",
