    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7ee72378-43bf-5cc6-817a-c82f9bebae25",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given $h(x,y,z)=4xy+2x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =4y+2{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =4y+2{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =4x+4xy+0 \\\\  & =4x+4xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4xy+2x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=(4y+2{y}^{2})\\,i+(4x+4xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4y+2{y}^{2})\\,i+(4x+4xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4y+2{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4x+4xy=0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(4+2y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=-2.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4x(-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-2,0).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "0c2dbaea-04e9-532c-8ce4-ae5b5ae85320",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
