  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124445,
      "id": "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340",
      "title": "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9af06e55-ac4b-5c12-8c18-1aa1d9b9f6fb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given $h(x,y,z)=3xy+x{y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy+x{y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =3y+{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =3y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy+x{y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =3x+2xy+0 \\\\  & =3x+2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3xy+x{y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+9{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=(3y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(3x+2xy)\\,j+9{z}^{2}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(3x+2xy)\\,j+9{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3y+{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ 3x+2xy=0 \\\\ 9{z}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(3+y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=-3.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2x(-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-3,0).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "dddbe0db-3fa2-56bc-8eee-1d8130ef9468",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
