      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{18}{xy}+\\frac{3}{y}+\\frac{6}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{6}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{18z+6y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{18}{xy}+\\frac{3}{y}+\\frac{6}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{18+3x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{18}{xy}+\\frac{3}{y}+\\frac{6}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{6+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{18z+6y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{18+3x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{6+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{18z+6y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{18+3x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{6+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{18z+6y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{18+3x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{6+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-6.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6+{z}^{2}(-6)}{{z}^{2}(-6)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-6$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{18z+6y}{(-6)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ 18z+6y & =0 \\\\ y & =-3z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-3$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=3.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-6,-3,1)$ and $(-6,3,-1).$",
