    "question": {
      ":question/id": "636f2e6b-5963-52d7-b0bd-f28358e69908",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given $h(x,y,z)=2xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =2y+{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =2x+2xy+0 \\\\  & =2x+2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=(2y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(2x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(2x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2y+{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ 2x+2xy=0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(2+y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=-2.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2x(-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-2,0).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
